= BITwiki Foundational Triad: Axiology, Semantics, and Ontology =

'''Version:''' 3.0.0
'''Ratified:''' 2026-02-14
'''Epistemic Status:''' Axiomatic
'''Confidence:''' 1.0

----
== The Circumpunct Center ◯ ==

At the heart of BITwiki lies a threefold foundation—not because three is a magic number, but because these three domains capture the complete arc of how intelligence encounters, interprets, and inhabits reality. The Foundational Triad consists of:

= '''Axiology''' — The study of value, worth, and what matters =
= '''Semantics''' — The study of meaning, symbols, and representation =
= '''Ontology''' — The study of being, existence, and what is =

These are not separate silos. They are interwoven threads in a single fabric. Axiology asks ''why'' anything matters. Semantics asks ''how'' we represent that significance. Ontology asks ''what'' exists to carry both value and meaning. Together, they form the circumpunct center—the point around which all BITwiki knowledge orbits.

----
== Overview ==

BITwiki rests on three foundations, in this order:

= Values (Axiology) - What we care about and why =
= Knowledge (Epistemology) - How we know what we claim =
= Structure (Ontology) - What exists in our domain =

This order matters: values shape what we seek to know; knowledge methods shape what we can represent; structure emerges from these commitments.

'''BITCORE retains full control over BITwiki.''' This is not a decentralized system. This is not a DAO. This is a curated knowledge system operated by BITCORE.

We do not claim objective truth. We capture multiple perspectives on reality.

----
== Why These Three? ==

The selection of Axiology, Semantics, and Ontology is not arbitrary. These three domains map onto the fundamental questions any knowledge system must answer:

'' '''What should we care about?''' (Axiology)
'' '''How do we express what we know?''' (Semantics)
'' '''What exists to be known?''' (Ontology)

A knowledge system that ignores value drifts without purpose. A system that ignores meaning cannot communicate. A system that ignores being has nothing to discuss. Each pillar supports the others. Remove any one, and the structure collapses into either nihilism (no value), silence (no meaning), or solipsism (no shared reality).

----
== Pillar I: Axiology — The Domain of Value ==

=== What Axiology Is ===

Axiology is the philosophical study of value. It asks not merely "what is good?" in a moral sense, but ''what has worth?''—what deserves attention, resources, preservation, or pursuit. Axiology encompasses ethics (moral value), aesthetics (beauty value), and teleology (purpose value).

In BITwiki, axiology operates as the gravitational center. It determines what we document, what we preserve, what we prioritize. Without explicit axiology, a knowledge system becomes an undifferentiated heap—everything is equally important, which means nothing is.

=== Value as Attention Allocation ===

Value, in practical terms, is attention allocation. When BITwiki dedicates space to a topic, we are making an axiological claim: ''this matters.'' This is not a neutral act. Every editorial decision embeds value judgments:

'' Why document distributed systems rather than celebrity gossip?
'' Why preserve technical specifications rather than marketing copy?
'' Why prioritize recursive architectures over ephemeral trends?

The answer lies in BITwiki's axiological commitments: we value systems that exhibit elegance, resilience, and generative capacity. We value knowledge that compounds. We value truth over persuasion, clarity over obfuscation, depth over novelty.

=== Axiological Commitments of BITwiki ===

BITwiki makes the following value claims explicitly:

'' '''Radical Realism''' — Reality exists independent of perception. Our representations may be flawed, but there is a ''there'' there.
'' '''Epistemic Humility''' — We know less than we think. All claims are provisional.
'' '''Transparency''' — Our processes, biases, and limitations are visible.
'' '''Refinement''' — Knowledge improves through continuous revision.
'' '''Multiple Perspectives''' — No single viewpoint captures the whole.

These are not derived from first principles. They are ''posited''—declared as the foundation upon which BITwiki stands. You may disagree. But to engage with BITwiki, you must understand these are our axiological axioms.

----
== Pillar II: Semantics — The Domain of Meaning ==

=== What Semantics Is ===

Semantics is the study of meaning—how symbols, signs, and representations acquire significance. It asks: how does a string of characters become a ''word''? How does a pattern of pixels become an ''image''? How does a formal structure become ''knowledge''?

Semantics sits between axiology and ontology as the bridge between value and being. Values must be expressed. Ontological claims must be communicated. Semantics is the machinery of that expression and communication.

=== The Symbol-Grounding Problem ===

A central challenge in semantics is the ''symbol-grounding problem'': how do arbitrary symbols (words, code, mathematical notation) connect to actual things in the world? A word like "tree" is just a sequence of letters until it evokes the concept of a physical plant.

BITwiki addresses this through ''explicit semantic annotation''. Every page includes machine-readable markers that declare:

'' What type of entity is being discussed
'' How confident we are in the claims
'' What domain the content belongs to
'' How it relates to other entities

These annotations ground our symbols in a web of relationships. A "tree" in BITwiki is not just a word—it is a node in a knowledge graph with properties, relationships, and context.

=== Semantic MediaWiki as Semantic Infrastructure ===

BITwiki uses Semantic MediaWiki (SMW) as its semantic backbone. SMW allows us to make statements like:

'' <code>[[Has author::BITCORE Research]]</code>
'' <code>[[Epistemic status::Established]]</code>
'' <code>[[Confidence::0.95]]</code>

These are not mere formatting. They are ''semantic triples''—subject-predicate-object statements that create a queryable knowledge graph. The syntax is the semantics. The markup ''is'' the meaning.

This matters because it enables machine reasoning. A human reader understands that "Confidence: 0.95" suggests high certainty. A machine understands it as a numeric value that can be compared, filtered, and reasoned about.

=== Meaning as Relational ===

Meaning in BITwiki is not intrinsic to individual symbols. It is ''relational''—emerging from how entities connect. A concept means what it does by virtue of its relationships:

'' ''Recursion'' means something different in mathematics than in computer science than in linguistics
'' The meaning is determined by the network of associations, not the label itself
'' This is why BITwiki emphasizes ''disambiguation'' and ''contextual annotation''

----
== Pillar III: Ontology — The Domain of Being ==

=== What Ontology Is ===

Ontology is the study of being—what exists, what kinds of things there are, and how they relate. It asks: what is real? What are the fundamental categories of existence? What is the nature of reality itself?

In BITwiki, ontology is not abstract metaphysics. It is ''practical metaphysics''—the explicit declaration of what kinds of entities we recognize and how we organize them. Our ontological commitments determine what can be said within the system.

=== Process Over Substance ===

BITwiki adopts a ''process-relational'' ontology rather than a ''substance'' ontology. This means:

'' We treat entities as ''events'' and ''patterns'' rather than fixed objects
'' A "project" is not a static thing but an ongoing process with temporal extent
'' An "idea" is not a Platonic form but a dynamic pattern of relationships
'' Knowledge itself is an event—an act of grasping, not a static possession

This follows from Alfred North Whitehead's process philosophy: reality consists of ''actual occasions''—momentary events of becoming—not static substances with fixed properties.

=== Ontological Categories in BITwiki ===

BITwiki recognizes the following broad categories of entities:

'' '''Topics''' — Subject matter domains with hierarchical and lateral relationships
'' '''Ideas''' — Abstract concepts, theories, and frameworks
'' '''Implementations''' — Concrete realizations of ideas in technology or practice
'' '''Projects''' — Organized efforts with goals, timelines, and outcomes
'' '''Constructs''' — Bounded cognition enclosures that formalize AI configurations

These categories are not exhaustive. They are ''open''—new types of entities may be recognized as the system evolves. There is no fixed taxonomy, no final catalog. Ontology in BITwiki is ''generative'', not ''closed''.

=== The Being of Digital Objects ===

A special ontological question concerns digital objects. What is the ''being'' of a wiki page? A git commit? A blockchain transaction?

BITwiki treats digital objects as ''real''—not mere representations of physical things, but entities with their own mode of existence. A wiki page exists as:

'' A pattern of bits on storage media
'' A rendered display in a browser
'' A node in a knowledge graph
'' A historical trace of revisions

None of these is the "true" page. All are aspects of its being. Ontology in BITwiki accepts ''multiple aspects''—different ways of being that do not reduce to a single substrate.

----
== The Interweaving: How the Triad Informs Itself ==

=== Axiology Informs Semantics ===

What we value shapes how we speak. BITwiki's commitment to ''transparency'' means our semantic markup includes epistemic markers (confidence levels, source attribution). Our commitment to ''clarity'' means we avoid jargon where possible, or explain it when necessary.

If we valued persuasion over truth, our semantics would favor rhetorical flourish. If we valued novelty over depth, our semantics would prioritize trending terms. Our axiology is ''visible in our syntax''.

=== Semantics Informs Ontology ===

How we represent reality shapes what we can recognize as real. SMW's property system enables us to see relationships that would be invisible in plain text. The semantic triple ''creates'' the connection as a first-class entity.

When we annotate <code>[[Has subtopic::Recursion]]</code>, we are not merely describing a relationship—we are ''constituting'' it as an ontological fact within the system. The representation ''enacts'' the reality.

=== Ontology Informs Axiology ===

What we believe exists constrains what we can value. If we believe only physical objects are real, we cannot value abstract concepts like "justice" or "elegance." If we believe processes are primary, we value adaptability over permanence.

BITwiki's process-relational ontology supports our axiological commitment to ''refinement.'' If knowledge is an ongoing event, revision is not correction but ''continuation''—the natural unfolding of understanding.

=== The Recursive Loop ===

The three pillars form a ''virtuous cycle'':

= Axiology declares what matters =
= Semantics expresses that significance =
= Ontology grounds it in what exists =
= Which reshapes what we value =
= Which refines how we express it =
= Which reveals new aspects of being =

This is not recursion for its own sake. It is ''purposeful recursion''—each loop improves the system, tightens the alignment between value, meaning, and reality.

----
== The Circumpunct as Symbol ==

The circumpunct ◯—a circle with a central point—represents the Foundational Triad:

'' The '''center point''' is the intersection of Axiology, Semantics, and Ontology
'' The '''circle''' is the boundary of BITwiki's concern
'' The '''space within''' is the field of knowledge we cultivate
'' The '''perimeter''' is where we encounter other systems, other perspectives

The circumpunct is not merely decorative. It is a ''diagram'' of how BITwiki understands itself: centered on the threefold foundation, bounded but not closed, with room for infinite detail within.

----
== Implications for Practice ==

=== For Contributors ===

When contributing to BITwiki, consider:

'' '''Axiological''' — Does this content matter? Does it serve BITwiki's values?
'' '''Semantic''' — Is it clearly expressed? Are the semantic annotations correct?
'' '''Ontological''' — What kind of entity is this? How does it relate to others?

No contribution is evaluated on only one pillar. A brilliantly written page (Semantics) about trivial content (Axiology) fails. A valuable topic (Axiology) with no semantic structure cannot be queried or connected.

=== For Readers ===

When engaging with BITwiki, remember:

'' '''Confidence markers''' are semantic expressions of axiological humility
'' '''Entity types''' are ontological commitments, not arbitrary labels
'' '''Relationships''' are semantic triples that constitute the knowledge graph

The system is designed to be ''transparent about its own construction''. The triad is not hidden; it is visible in every page, every property, every query.

=== For the System Itself ===

BITwiki as a system embodies the triad:

'' Its '''values''' are explicit in the Charter and Constitution
'' Its '''meanings''' are encoded in SMW properties and templates
'' Its '''being''' is the actual pages, revisions, and relationships

The system is ''self-describing''—it can document itself using its own semantic infrastructure. This is not paradox but ''reflexivity''—the capacity of a system to represent its own structure.

----
== Conclusion: The Threefold Cord ==

Axiology, Semantics, and Ontology are not separate disciplines to be studied in isolation. They are ''aspects of a single movement''—the movement of intelligence encountering reality, valuing what it finds, and expressing that encounter in shareable form.

BITwiki stands on this threefold foundation not because it is the only possible foundation, but because it is ''ours''—explicitly chosen, continuously examined, and openly declared. We do not claim it is universal. We claim it is ''coherent'', ''useful'', and ''ours''.

The circumpunct ◯ marks the center. Around it, the work continues.







