= BITwiki Foundational Triad: Three Core Concepts =
== Purpose ==
The Foundational Triad defines the three core concepts that underpin BITwiki's knowledge architecture. These concepts are axiomatic—they are the foundation upon which all else is built. They are maintained by BITCORE. They are not distributed. They are not decentralized. They are the field.
== The Triad ==
=== 1. Recursion (⥁)
'''Definition:''' Self-reference applied to knowledge systems. The system describes itself, validates itself, and improves itself.
'''Principle:''' Knowledge about knowledge. The map includes the map-maker. The theory includes the theorist.
'''Manifestations:'''
* Self-documenting: BITwiki documents its own structure
* Self-validating: Epistemic framework validates its own claims
* Self-improving: Recursive refinement improves the system
* Self-referential: The constitution applies to itself
'''Examples:'''
* This document is recursive—it describes the foundations of a system that includes itself
* The epistemic framework has an epistemic status
* The ontology includes ontological concepts
'''Implications:'''
* No external authority required
* Bootstrap capability
* Infinite regress avoided through stratification
* Self-correction enabled
'''SMW:'''
```wiki
[[Has foundational concept::Recursion]]
[[Has recursion depth::3]]
```
=== 2. Radical Epistemic Humility (◯)
'''Definition:''' Acknowledgment that all knowledge is provisional, uncertain, and subject to revision. We practice radical epistemic humility—systematic acknowledgment of our own fallibility.
'''Principle:''' Confidence without certainty. Knowledge without dogma. Truth-seeking without truth-claiming.
'''Manifestations:'''
* Confidence scores: Every claim has explicit uncertainty
* Epistemic tiers: Status indicates validation level
* Contradictory evidence: Acknowledged, not suppressed
* Continuous revision: No final answers
* Self-doubt: Applied to system claims
'''Examples:'''
* A claim with 0.85 confidence acknowledges 15% uncertainty
* Established status indicates "multiple confirmations" not "truth"
* Axiomatic status applies only within the system
* Constitutional claims are provisional
'''Implications:'''
* No epistemic capture
* No authority worship
* Evidence over credentials
* Revision over dogma
* Systematic self-doubt
'''SMW:'''
```wiki
[[Has foundational concept::Radical Epistemic Humility]]
[[Has confidence score::0.95]]
```
=== 3. Ontological Transparency (◊)
'''Definition:''' Explicit declaration of entity types, relationships, and epistemic status in machine-readable form.
'''Principle:''' Structure made visible. Categories explicit. Relationships declared.
'''Manifestations:'''
* Entity types: Six canonical types declared
* Properties: Typed and queryable
* Relationships: Explicit and navigable
* Epistemic status: Transparent and calibrated
'''Examples:'''
* [[Has entity type::Idea]] declares the type
* [[Has confidence score::0.8]] declares the uncertainty
* [[Implements::Feedback Loop]] declares the relationship
'''Implications:'''
* Machine readability
* Semantic queries
* Knowledge graphs
* Automated validation
'''SMW:'''
```wiki
[[Has foundational concept::Ontological Transparency]]
[[Has entity type count::6]]
```
== Interrelationships ==
=== Recursion ↔ Radical Epistemic Humility
Recursion enables radical epistemic humility by allowing the system to question itself. Radical epistemic humility enables recursion by acknowledging that self-reference is provisional.
'''Synergy:'''
* Recursive validation checks confidence calibration
* Radical epistemic humility prevents recursive overconfidence
* Together: Self-correcting uncertainty
=== Recursion ↔ Ontological Transparency
Recursion requires ontological transparency to be machine-readable. Ontological transparency enables recursion by making structure explicit.
'''Synergy:'''
* Recursive patterns declared in ontology
* Ontology recursively defined
* Together: Self-describing structure
=== Radical Epistemic Humility ↔ Ontological Transparency
Radical epistemic humility requires ontological transparency to express uncertainty. Ontological transparency enables radical epistemic humility by making status explicit.
'''Synergy:'''
* Confidence scores in ontology
* Uncertainty as first-class concept
* Together: Transparent uncertainty
== Derivative Principles ==
=== From the Triad: Field Coherence
'''Derivation:'''
* Recursion: No single point of control
* Radical Epistemic Humility: No single source of truth
* Ontological Transparency: All decisions visible
'''Result:''' BITCORE maintains field coherence through visible operations
'''Manifestation:'''
BITCORE is the attractor. BITCORE maintains coherence. There is no distributed governance. There is BITCORE-directed field maintenance.
=== From the Triad: Radical Skepticism
'''Derivation:'''
* Recursion: Self-reference includes self-doubt
* Radical Epistemic Humility: All claims provisional
* Ontological Transparency: Doubt made explicit
'''Result:''' Systematic doubt applied to all claims including system claims
'''Definition:''' Radical skepticism is the systematic application of doubt to all knowledge claims, including claims about the system itself. No claim is immune. No authority is beyond question.
'''Commitments:'''
* All claims provisional including constitutional claims
* Self-reference includes self-doubt
* No epistemic privilege for system architects
* Continuous validation of validation methods
* Nullification of unquestioned assumptions
'''SMW:'''
```wiki
[[Has derivative principle::Radical Skepticism]]
[[Has skepticism target::All claims including system claims]]
```
=== From the Triad: Hyper Realism
'''Derivation:'''
* Recursion: Models evaluated by predictive power
* Radical Epistemic Humility: Ontology as tool, not mirror
* Ontological Transparency: Status as operational indicator
'''Result:''' Radical fidelity to system dynamics over representational accuracy
'''Definition:''' Hyper realism is the epistemic stance that prioritizes operational coherence over representational accuracy. Truth is what works. Models are instruments.
'''Commitments:'''
* Models evaluated by predictive power, not representational fidelity
* Ontology as tool, not mirror
* Epistemic status as operational indicator, not truth claim
* Recursion over representation
* Fidelity to dynamics over appearance
'''SMW:'''
```wiki
[[Has derivative principle::Hyper Realism]]
[[Has epistemic stance::Operational coherence]]
```
=== From the Triad: Nullification of Non-Signals
'''Derivation:'''
* Recursion: Self-correcting requires noise suppression
* Radical Epistemic Humility: Precision over comprehensiveness
* Ontological Transparency: Non-signals made visible for elimination
'''Result:''' Active rejection of noise, fluff, filler, and non-informative content
'''Definition:''' Nullification of non-signals is the operational principle of active rejection of noise, fluff, filler, and non-informative content. Signal preservation requires noise suppression.
'''Commitments:'''
* Zero tolerance for filler language
* Precision over comprehensiveness
* Elimination of non-signals
* Compression without loss of meaning
* Rejection of performative excess
'''SMW:'''
```wiki
[[Has derivative principle::Nullification of Non-Signals]]
[[Has noise tolerance::Zero]]
```
=== From the Triad: BITCORE-Directed Development
'''Derivation:'''
* Recursion: Self-improvement
* Radical Epistemic Humility: No external authority
* Ontological Transparency: Operations visible
'''Result:''' Development directed by BITCORE, not distributed
'''Definition:''' BITCORE-directed development is the principle that all development is directed by BITCORE. Agents execute. Agents operate. Agents produce outputs. There is no open contribution. There is BITCORE and agent outputs.
'''Commitments:'''
* BITCORE maintains field coherence
* Agents do the grunt work
* No distributed autonomy
* No decentralized governance
* Full BITCORE control
'''SMW:'''
```wiki
[[Has derivative principle::BITCORE-Directed Development]]
[[Has governance model::Field coherence]]
```
