= BITwiki Foundational Triad =
Three concepts underpin everything BITwiki builds. Not as decoration. As load-bearing structure. We call them the Foundational Triad: Axiology, Semantics, Axology. Each addresses a distinct dimension of knowledge. Together, they form the coordinate system within which BITwiki operates.
== The Triad ==
=== Axiology (Value) ===
'''What we mean:''' The study of value—what makes knowledge artifacts worth cultivating, worth preserving, worth transmitting.
Axiology asks: Why does this matter? Not in vague inspirational terms. In operational terms. What problems does this solve? What understanding does it enable? What research does it provoke?
Consider the difference between two claims about consciousness. One: "Consciousness is a quantum phenomenon in microtubules." Another: "Consciousness emerges from integrated information processing." Both are hypotheses. Both have limited evidence. But their axiological value differs. The first has driven specific experimental programs, generated testable predictions about quantum effects in biological systems. The second has spawned a research program with mathematical frameworks and clinical applications. We evaluate not just truth value but generative capacity.
Axiology is not subjectivism. Some things are genuinely more valuable than others. A framework that generates predictions is more valuable than one that merely explains post-hoc. A concept that connects disparate domains is more valuable than an isolated insight. But value is contextual. What serves one inquiry may obstruct another.
In BITwiki, axiology manifests as explicit evaluation of knowledge artifacts. We ask: What is the value of this claim? What would increase or decrease its value? How does it relate to other valuable artifacts?
'''Example in practice:''' When we encounter a new hypothesis about neural function, we do not ask only "Is this true?" We ask: What would this explain? What would it predict? What research would it enable? What would falsify it? The hypothesis has value proportional to its generative capacity.
=== Semantics (Meaning) ===
'''What we mean:''' The study of meaning—how symbols, concepts, and structures carry significance, how they refer, how they combine to form complex expressions.
Semantics asks: What does this mean? Not in dictionary definition terms. In operational terms. How does this concept function? What does it connect to? What inferences does it enable?
Consider the word "recursion." In mathematics, it means a function defined in terms of itself. In linguistics, it means the capacity to embed clauses within clauses. In computer science, it means a procedure that calls itself. These meanings are related but distinct. The semantic field of "recursion" includes all of them, with their overlaps and divergences.
In BITwiki, semantics manifests as explicit ontological structure. We declare entity types not to impose categories but to make meaning machine-readable. [[Has entity type::Idea]] tells us how this concept functions in the knowledge ecosystem. [[Implements::Recursion]] tells us what it operationalizes.
Semantics is not just about definitions. It is about relationships. A concept means what it does—what it connects to, what it enables, what it constrains. Meaning is use, as Wittgenstein argued. But use is pattern, and patterns can be made explicit.
'''Example in practice:''' When we define "emergence," we do not offer a single definition. We trace its semantic field—how it functions in complexity science, in philosophy of mind, in sociology. We mark the overlaps and divergences. We make the meaning navigable.
=== Axology (Action) ===
'''What we mean:''' The study of action—how knowledge translates into practice, how understanding becomes operation, how theory becomes intervention.
Axology asks: What does this do? Not in abstract terms. In concrete terms. How does this knowledge change behavior? What practices does it enable? What outcomes does it produce?
Consider the theory of evolution by natural selection. It is a semantic structure—a set of concepts and relationships. It has axiological value—generating research, explaining diversity. But its axological dimension is equally important. It changed how we breed crops, how we develop medicine, how we understand disease. The theory became operational.
In BITwiki, axology manifests as attention to implementation. We do not stop at ideas. We trace how ideas become technologies, methods, practices. We document not just what is known but what is done.
Axology is not applied versus pure research. All knowledge has axological dimensions. Even the most abstract mathematics shapes how we think, what we can conceive, what we can build. The question is not whether knowledge does something but what it does.
'''Example in practice:''' When we examine a concept like "feedback loop," we trace its axological trajectory—from cybernetic theory to thermostat design to organizational management to climate modeling. The concept does work across domains. Understanding that work is understanding the concept.
== Interrelationships ==
The Triad is not three separate concerns. They interpenetrate. Each implies the others.
'''Axiology without Semantics is empty.''' We cannot evaluate value without understanding meaning. What does this concept do? We must know what it is before we can assess what it does.
'''Semantics without Axology is blind.''' We cannot understand meaning without seeing operation. What does this concept mean? We must see how it functions to know what it signifies.
'''Axology without Axiology is aimless.''' We cannot guide action without evaluating worth. What should we do? We must know what is valuable to choose what to pursue.
Consider a concrete example: the concept of "neural network."
'''Semantically:''' It means a computational model inspired by biological neurons, with layers of interconnected nodes, weights adjusted through training. This meaning connects to related concepts—machine learning, backpropagation, deep learning, artificial intelligence.
'''Axiologically:''' It has value because it enables pattern recognition, prediction, classification across domains. It generates research programs, commercial applications, theoretical insights. Its value is contextual—high for image recognition, lower for symbolic reasoning.
'''Axologically:''' It becomes operational in facial recognition systems, medical diagnosis tools, language models, autonomous vehicles. The concept translates into specific implementations with specific effects.
Understanding neural networks requires all three dimensions. Semantics without axiology misses what they do. Axology without semantics misses how they work. Axiology without axiology misses why they matter.
== Derivative Principles ==
From the Triad, several operational principles follow:
'''Radical Epistemic Humility''' derives from Axiology. If value is contextual, then certainty is misplaced. What serves one inquiry may fail another. We acknowledge uncertainty not as weakness but as precision.
'''Ontological Transparency''' derives from Semantics. If meaning is relational, then structure must be explicit. We declare entity types, mark relationships, make the semantic field navigable.
'''Scoped Recursion''' derives from Axology. If knowledge does work, then self-reference must serve function. We apply BITwiki to itself not for display but for improvement.
'''Nullification of Non-Signals''' derives from all three. Content without semantic structure, without axiological value, without axological effect—this is noise. We reject it.
== SMW Implementation ==
The Triad manifests in SMW through explicit properties:
```wiki
[[Has foundational concept::Axiology]]
[[Has foundational concept::Semantics]]
[[Has foundational concept::Axology]]
[[Has triad completeness::true]]
```
Queries can discover Triad-complete pages:
```wiki
